<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Three specs decide it: weight, shaft flex and balance.</strong> For most adults buying a first or second club racket that means 4U or 5U, a medium or flexible shaft, and even balance, and you can buy all of it for under £80. Above about £80 the market starts selling you a professional's racket instead: heavier in the head, stiffer in the shaft, and increasingly with no strings in the box.</p>

      <p>Get it wrong and you will spend £150 finding out that the racket was never the thing holding you back.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Buy 4U or 5U, medium or flexible shaft, even balance.</strong> That is the safe default for a first or second club racket, and the cheapest racket we found carrying all of it was £24.95.</li>
        <li><strong>Under £80 is the band.</strong> Above roughly £80 the specs do not stop improving, they start pointing at somebody else.</li>
        <li><strong>Check whether it comes strung.</strong> Above £120 most rackets do not, and a restring is another £18 to £30.</li>
        <li><strong>Moving mass towards the head did not increase shuttle speed</strong> in a controlled study of twenty experienced players. That is measured, not opinion.</li>
        <li><strong>The racket is not your limiting factor.</strong> Where you hit the shuttle and how fast you swing account for most of it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for adults buying a first or second badminton racket in the UK, playing club or social badminton. Market figures come from our own count of 228 adult rackets in Badminton HQ's catalogue on 23 August 2026, with the trends checked against Central Sports. Specifications are the retailers' own product tags describing how each racket is marketed, not measurements we took. <strong>We have no commercial relationship with any racket manufacturer or retailer, and no link on this page earns us a commission.</strong> The full method is at the foot of this guide.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#which-specs">Which specs actually decide it?</a>
      <a href="#spend">How much should you spend?</a>
      <a href="#cheap">What does the cheap shelf actually sell you?</a>
      <a href="#laws">What do the Laws require?</a>
      <a href="#weight">Weight: what do 3U, 4U and 5U mean?</a>
      <a href="#balance">Does a head-heavy racket make you hit harder?</a>
      <a href="#flex">Shaft flex: stiff or flexible?</a>
      <a href="#strung">Does it come strung?</a>
      <a href="#grip">Grip size: does it matter?</a>
      <a href="#ignore">What should you ignore?</a>
      <a href="#demo">Can you try before you buy?</a>
      <a href="#does-it-matter">Does the racket matter as much as you think?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
