    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="which-specs">Which specs actually decide it?</h2>
      <p>Three specs decide it: <strong>weight, shaft flex and balance</strong>. Manufacturers publish five fields, adding grip size and a maximum string tension, and the buying guides walk you through them as though they were five decisions. They are not.</p>
      <p>Grip size is a fit check rather than a choice, and the UK market largely makes it for you. String tension is a real decision but not one you make when you buy, because it recurs every few months and because under about £100 the racket almost always arrives already strung. That leaves three.</p>
      <p>There is a sixth thing that matters more than two of the five and appears on nobody's spec sheet: <strong>whether the racket comes with strings in it at all.</strong></p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/racket-anatomy.svg" alt="A badminton racket shown face-on with its parts labelled: the grip, the shaft, the throat and the stringed head. Balance is marked as a single distance in millimetres from the butt of the handle. Under 285 millimetres is head-light, 285 to 295 is even, and over 295 is head-heavy, so the whole distinction spans one centimetre of a 675 millimetre racket." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Balance is a measured distance from the butt of the handle, not a style, and head-light to head-heavy is a centimetre of it. Everything a retailer lists describes this frame; only three of the numbers change your decision.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="spend">How much should you spend?</h2>
      <p><strong>Spend under £80 on the racket.</strong> Somewhere between £25 and £80 you can buy a lighter frame, even balance, a shaft that is not stiff, and strings in the box, which is the whole specification we recommend here. In the notation the listings use, explained below, that is 4U or 5U with even balance and a medium or flexible shaft. Between there and £250 nothing on that list improves.</p>
      <p>We counted 228 adult rackets in one UK specialist retailer's catalogue to check this, and cross-checked the direction of travel against a second. Plenty of guides will name you a price. What none of the ones we checked does is count what the market actually stocks at each one.</p>
      <p>Once you count, what the catalogue shows is not a plateau. It is a market that steadily stops selling you the thing you want.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" style="overflow-x:auto">
      <table class="cmp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Price band</th><th>Rackets</th><th>4U or 5U</th><th>Head-heavy</th><th>Stiff shaft</th><th>Marketed to beginners</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Under £30</td><td>26</td><td>4%</td><td>19%</td><td>8%</td><td>92%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£30-49</td><td>33</td><td>73%</td><td>30%</td><td>6%</td><td>58%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£50-79</td><td>32</td><td>75%</td><td>34%</td><td>9%</td><td>31%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£80-119</td><td>57</td><td>65%</td><td>54%</td><td>18%</td><td>0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£120-179</td><td>58</td><td>66%</td><td>55%</td><td>40%</td><td>0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£180+</td><td>22</td><td>45%</td><td>64%</td><td>73%</td><td>0%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p>Count the rackets that match the whole club specification at once, and the picture is starker. Sixteen of the 228 are 4U or 5U with even balance, a medium or flexible shaft, and strings included. <strong>Thirteen of those sixteen cost under £80, and they start at £24.95. None of the twenty-two rackets above £180 match, and not one racket above £80 is marketed to a beginner at all.</strong></p>
      <p>There is no conspiracy in this. Premium rackets are marketed to and through professionals, and a professional's racket is stiffer, heavier in the head, and sold unstrung so the player can choose their own tension. The £200 racket is better at being a professional's racket. The mistake is assuming that axis points at you.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/racket-price-curve.svg" alt="Two charts against price band. The first counts rackets matching the full club specification of 4U or 5U weight, even balance, medium or flexible shaft and strings included: it peaks at seven rackets in the fifty to seventy-nine pound band, and thirteen of the sixteen matches fall under eighty pounds, with none at all above a hundred and eighty. The second shows the share of rackets sold as a frame only with no strings, rising steadily from zero per cent below sixty pounds to one hundred per cent above a hundred and eighty." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">228 adult rackets in Badminton HQ's catalogue, 23 August 2026. The club specification is concentrated under £80, and the strings come out of the box as the price climbs.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="cheap">What does the cheap shelf actually sell you?</h2>
      <p>The cheap shelf sells you a heavier racket, which is the opposite of what most people assume. Of the twenty rackets under £24 we counted, seventeen were 1U or 2U, meaning 90 g and up, including budget lines from brands you have heard of. The first 4U appears at £24.95, and between £30 and £80 about three rackets in four are 4U or 5U.</p>
      <p>So the concrete upgrade from a £15 supermarket racket is not carbon fibre or a bigger sweet spot. It is dropping ten to fifteen grams off a frame of about ninety, which is enough to make the racket noticeably easier to accelerate and reposition.</p>

      <h2 id="laws">What do the Laws require?</h2>
      <p>The Laws of Badminton regulate whether a racket is legal, and almost nothing about how it plays. Law 4 caps the frame at 680 mm long and 230 mm wide and the stringed area at 280 mm by 220 mm, and requires the racket to be <em>"free of attached objects and protrusions"</em> beyond those limiting wear, vibration or weight distribution. That is essentially the whole of it.</p>
      <p>The Laws set no limit on weight, balance, shaft stiffness, grip size or string tension. Every number printed on the shaft is a manufacturer's own convention, and that matters because you will read that the U-rating is a BWF classification. It is not, and Law 4 is the proof: there is no governing-body standard for how heavy a racket may be, so two brands can label the same weight differently and both be correct.</p>

      <h2 id="weight">Weight: what do 3U, 4U and 5U mean?</h2>
      <p>The U number is an unstrung weight band, and it runs backwards: <strong>3U is 85 to 89 g, 4U is 80 to 84 g, 5U is 75 to 79 g.</strong> Lower number, heavier racket. Buy 4U, or 5U if you would rather have the easier racket to swing.</p>
      <p><strong>The trap is that none of this is standardised.</strong> A second notation using W is in circulation, and it counts the other way, so a higher number means a heavier racket. The catalogue we read tags both at once, and is not consistent about it: W1 appears against 5U on some listings and against 6U on others. The two scales coincide at 3, where 3U and W3 are both 85 to 89 g.</p>
      <p>They do not coincide at 2: a 2U is 90 to 94 g and a W2 is 80 to 84 g, so <strong>two rackets both labelled 2 are about ten grams apart.</strong></p>
      <p>The gram ranges behind the U numbers are a convention rather than a standard, and not every brand uses the same ones. Victor publishes 4U as 80.0 to 84.9 g; Apacs lists its 4U frames at around 84 g.</p>
      <p>A 4U is not a fixed weight. It is a fixed word, and the only way to know what it means on a particular racket is to read the gram range printed next to it.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" style="overflow-x:auto">
      <table class="cmp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Unstrung weight</th><th>U scale</th><th>W scale</th><th>Who it suits</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>75-79 g</td><td>5U</td><td>W1</td><td>Easiest to swing. Fine for anyone, and the safer choice if you are unsure.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80-84 g</td><td>4U</td><td>W2</td><td>The club default, and what about three rackets in four between £30 and £80 are.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>85-89 g</td><td>3U</td><td>W3</td><td>Heavier. Wants a developed swing to be worth carrying.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="balance">Does a head-heavy racket make you hit harder?</h2>
      <p>Moving more of a racket’s mass towards the head did not make the shuttle leave faster in the one controlled experiment we could find, and that is measured rather than argued. A 2023 study in <em>Scientific Reports</em> had twenty experienced players hit 1,500 smashes with five rackets of <strong>identical mass</strong> (93.8 to 94.1 g) whose weight distribution was varied across a range wider than the commercial one. As the weight moved towards the head, racket head speed fell significantly. Shuttle speed did not follow it down.</p>
      <p>Balance itself is just a distance: the point along the racket where it balances, measured in millimetres from the butt of the handle. The convention across UK retailers and coaching sites is that under 285 mm is head-light, 285 to 295 mm is even, and over 295 mm is head-heavy. <strong>The whole argument is about a centimetre.</strong></p>
      <p>What that centimetre changes is how the racket feels to swing, not how fast the shuttle leaves it. The consensus across coaching and retailer guidance, which is description rather than measurement, is that head-heavy feels solid and planted and is more tiring over a long evening, while head-light feels quick in the hand and less stable through a clear. Even balance is the sensible default, and it is what Victor's own beginner guidance recommends for a player without a settled style.</p>
      <p>Two honest limits on that study. It held mass constant, so it tells you about <em>where</em> the weight sits and not about how much there is. And its players were regional to international standard, so it says nothing directly about a club player's swing.</p>

      <h2 id="flex">Shaft flex: stiff or flexible?</h2>
      <p>Buy a medium or flexible shaft, and know that the manufacturers openly disagree about which. Yonex's entry tier is mostly built around a flexible shaft, though not all of it; Victor's own beginner guide recommends 4 to 7 on a one-to-ten handling scale, which is moderate stiffness rather than flex. There is no cross-brand standard, and no way to convert one maker's label into another's.</p>
      <p>The reason it matters is that a shaft bends and then straightens during the swing, and that adds racket head speed. A study of eight national and international players found shaft deflection increased head velocity by 13.2% during a full smash, with the effect depending on the relationship between the player, the racket's mass distribution and the shaft. Eight players is a small sample, and we have not found a larger one. </p>
      <p>A stiffer shaft demands more consistent timing to exploit, which is why manufacturers steer developing players towards less-stiff frames and stronger players towards stiffer ones. No research has established a swing speed at which one becomes better than the other.</p>
      <p>Be clear about what that is and is not. The research explains why flex matters; it does not establish an optimal flex for a recreational player, and nobody has tested one. Medium or flexible is a conservative buying recommendation built on the manufacturers' own beginner guidance, not a measured optimum.</p>

      <h2 id="strung">Does it come strung?</h2>
      <p>Check whether the racket comes with strings before you compare it on price, because above about £120 most rackets do not come with strings, and it is easy to miss: it sits in the product title or the tag list as "Frame Only" or "String Included", not in the spec table with the weight and the balance. Of the rackets whose listing states one way or the other, the share sold frame-only rose steadily with price: 5% below £100, 39% at £100 to £119, 60% at £120 to £139, and all 22 above £180.</p>
      <p>Add a restring and the price gap between a frame-only racket and a strung one widens. So a £180 frame is not £180, and you are choosing a tension on no evidence, because you have not yet hit anything with the racket. </p>
      <p>One caveat worth checking before you assume the worst: some retailers discount or include the first restring on dearer frames. Of the two we counted, one advertises free restringing on "most premium rackets" and had it on 25 Yonex models from £112 up, and the other offers half price over £100. Neither is a rule you can assume; both are worth two minutes before you pay for a restring separately.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Worked example: what a £180 racket actually costs</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">£184 for the frame, the cheapest in our count above £180. It arrives unstrung, because up there every racket we counted does. A restring runs about £18 to £30 with the string included, going by the published price lists of seven UK stringers, so unless the retailer includes one, call it £205. You pick a tension you have no basis for picking, because you have never hit a shuttle with this frame.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">The £50 racket next to it arrives strung, playable that evening, and its published specs are the ones that suit you. The difference, £155, is several years of restringing, or a pair of court shoes and change.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="grip">Grip size: does it matter?</h2>
      <p>Grip size matters less than the amount written about it suggests, and the market has largely decided for you. Of the 228 adult rackets in our count, 185 stated a grip size, and 107 of those were G5 against 41 G4 and 36 G6. G5 is the default you will be offered unless you go looking. A typical overgrip is about half a millimetre thick, which wraps to roughly three millimetres of extra circumference, which is near enough one full size step on its own.</p>
      <p>The evidence is split, and it is also borrowed. The two studies people cite here are <strong>tennis</strong> studies, and tennis grips start about an inch larger than badminton ones, so read them as suggestive rather than as findings about your racket. </p>
      <p>A 2006 study of sixteen collegiate players found that altering grip size within a quarter of an inch of the recommended fit did not significantly change forearm muscle activity, and may not be a meaningful risk factor for tennis elbow. A 2014 study found the opposite kind of result: an optimal grip diameter that measurably reduces the grip force required, and with it the load on the extensor tendons. The two disagree, which is the honest state of it.</p>
      <p>Nobody should tell you with confidence that your grip size caused your elbow pain.</p>

      <h2 id="ignore">What should you ignore?</h2>
      <p>Four things take up most of the space on a racket listing and should change nothing about your decision.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Isometric heads.</strong> The squarer head shape is a real design, and Yonex claims a larger sweet spot for it against a conventional round frame. It is also thirty years old and now effectively universal, so it does not tell you anything about the rackets you are choosing between.</li>
        <li><strong>Signature editions.</strong> Often a player's name and a new colourway on a frame that already exists. One retailer says so itself, describing a signature series as carrying "exclusive colourways while maintaining the same performance specifications as the standard models", and the published spec tables for the pair we compared match on weight, balance, flex, tension and frame material. Not every signature frame is only a colourway, so compare it against the base model before paying the premium. The most expensive racket we counted was one, at £251.95.</li>
        <li><strong>"Match the racket to your playing style."</strong> Reasonable advice for somebody who has one. If you are buying a first or second racket you do not yet, and the manufacturers' own answer for that case is even balance.</li>
        <li><strong>Material names.</strong> Above about £30 the frame will be graphite with something added, and the something is not comparable between brands. Neither catalogue we read tags material at all, which tells you how much weight the retailers put on it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>One number on the spec sheet is worth respecting: the <strong>maximum recommended string tension</strong>. Of the rackets in our count that stated a limit, most were rated to 28 lb. Treat it as the manufacturer's warranty line. Stringers will go above it on request, and a manufacturer is unlikely to honour a frame that breaks above it.</p>
      <p>If you are buying strung and have no idea what tension to ask for, the honest answer is that you do not need one yet: take whatever the racket ships with, play a term, and only change it when you can say what you want to be different. Low tension gives more power and a larger margin for a mis-hit; high tension gives more control and punishes one. That is the whole trade, and you cannot judge which side you want before you have hit anything.</p>

      <h2 id="demo">Can you try before you buy?</h2>
      <p>Several UK retailers run demo schemes, and the terms differ more than you would expect. Of the five publishing terms when we looked, every one takes either a deposit for the full price of the racket, refunded minus a fee, or a flat hire charge. Four cap how many you can hold at once, usually at two or three. After that they diverge: one excludes whole brands and requires the plastic sleeve to stay on the original grip, another allows unlimited swaps for the postage, and the return windows run from ten working days to fourteen.</p>
      <p>Read the one you are using rather than assuming, and check whether the fee comes off a purchase.</p>
      <p>It is worth doing if you are choosing between two rackets you have narrowed down. It is not worth doing to discover whether you like badminton rackets. And your club is the cheaper option: most club nights contain several people who will happily let you hit six shots with theirs.</p>

      <h2 id="does-it-matter">Does the racket matter as much as you think?</h2>
      <p>The racket matters much less than the amount of advice about it implies. Across 2,386 smashes by 65 international players, where the shuttle struck the strings accounted for about a quarter of the variation in shuttle speed on its own. A separate study of 297 jump smashes at the 2016 All England and 2017 World Championships put impact location and racket head speed together at 89% of it. Where you hit it and how fast you swing is the game, and the spec sheet is a rounding error on that.</p>
      <p>It is also worth saying plainly that <strong>nobody has measured any of this at club level.</strong> The badminton studies here used regional to international players, and the two grip studies were done on tennis players. They are the best evidence available and they point the same way, but a recreational badminton player is not who was tested.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>What to check on the listing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Four things, in this order. Everything else on the page is describing the racket rather than helping you choose it.</p>
        <ol style="margin:12px 0 0">
          <li>Weight: 4U or 5U (Li-Ning: W2 or W1).</li>
          <li>Shaft: medium or flexible.</li>
          <li>Balance: even.</li>
          <li>Strings: included, not frame-only.</li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">All four true and under £80: buy it and go and play. <strong>Expect to have to hunt.</strong> Only sixteen rackets in the whole catalogue we counted carried all four, so you will more often be dropping one of them.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">Drop balance first: it is the one spec we have a controlled study saying does not change how fast the shuttle leaves. Drop the strings requirement second, and add the cost of a restring before you compare. Hold on to the weight and the shaft for as long as you can, and to the weight longest of all: it is the one you notice on every swing, and the one the cheapest rackets most often get wrong.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Check whether the price includes strings. It is the one thing that changes what you actually pay, it is rarely next to the price, and above £180 the answer was no for all 22 rackets we counted.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Fair games, without the admin</h4>
        <p>Whatever you are holding, the evening works better when the games are even. ePegboard picks balanced pairings on a club night and keeps the mixing varied, so people play with a range of partners rather than the same three. Free for clubs.</p>
